WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
Tecmo Baseball!

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.860
Manage your own team to the series championship.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.240
Even create your own all-star game.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:29.280
NOSTALGIA And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:30.940
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.220
I'm Joe.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.620
And I'm Sam.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:42.200
And we are playing another NES baseball game, yet somehow still not getting like a sequel.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.260
This is just another different company saying, I want to stay up at baseball games too.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.880
There will be no monopoly on baseball games, but there will be monopoly on the NES.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:53.940
We just haven't gotten there yet.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:55.780
Guys, thoughts on baseball games.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.720
Take it away, Sean. NOSTALGIA

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.720
We've seen a lot of different basic takes on it.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.220
Like where to place the camera,

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.480
what to do with hitting and pitching.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.980
There's a couple things that have sort of become universal,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:20.640
but since there's no recipe for a sports game yet, they all kind of have their own flavor to them.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.660
NOSTALGIA Joe, give me your thoughts on going to a baseball game.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.600
NOSTALGIA I like going to a baseball game.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.040
It's a great, uh, a great pastime, an American pastime.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.800
Uh, I, I, and this might be a bold stance, but I actually prefer going to a baseball game

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.180
than watching two AI opponents play each other on the NES.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.700
Ah, it's a little too far for me.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.000
NOSTALGIA Yeah, I know, I know. It's a, it's an unpopular opinion.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.640
NOSTALGIA Sam, thoughts on cricket as a rival to baseball?

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.880
NOSTALGIA I don't think that it's really a rival to baseball.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.640
I see it more as a rival to running bases.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.860
Um, you know, the game that you'd play as a kid or as an adult, I don't really tell you how to live your life.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.020
But, um...

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.000
NOSTALGIA What is running bases?

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.740
NOSTALGIA You've never played running bases?

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.460
NOSTALGIA I've never played running bases.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.460
Unless I called it a different name or something.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.880
NOSTALGIA Should we get into this?

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:09.840
NOSTALGIA I would love to.

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:13.260
Sam, break down, break down just the basic rules of running bases.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.600
NOSTALGIA Okay, so the basic rules of running bases are there are two bases.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:18.500
And there are,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.660
you have, there are people, one person, two people on the bases.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.000
NOSTALGIA This is hotbox. One person on each base.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.280
Right? And you have, I think, usually a tennis ball.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:31.100
Uh, and you throw it back and forth.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.760
Those two people throw it back and forth.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:39.560
And each time you, they have three throws, I think.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.420
I think it's like one for the money, two for the show.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:42.840
Three, get ready, four to go.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.040
Um, that's how we would play it.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:53.620
And then on the last one, you have, so if you're, there's also people who are on the bases and you have to run to the other base.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:03:01.280
And if you start running to the other base, the, uh, the, either person with the ball can peg it at you.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.480
And if you get, uh, hit with the ball, then you're out.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:09.580
So some people go immediately to try and get people off guard.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.260
And some people wait until you have to go on the fourth one.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.120
And there's a big crowd of people.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.780
So you're kind of protected in the herd.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.240
Um, and some people might wait for someone to try and, you know, throw the ball.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.960
And they mess up the throw and it goes too far and they get that, they get away that way.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.100
But that, that's kind of it.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.560
It's kind of like monkey in the middle, but I'll just know you're trying to hit the monkey.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.120
From that description.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.020
I don't think it's just from your description.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:38.040
I think, I think that I just don't have a handle on that.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.760
I can't even visualize what's happening.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.520
It's basically a base running.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.820
I'm pretty sure that I played this just by a different name.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.460
Uh, we just called it hot box.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.900
We also didn't, didn't say whatever it is you said at the top.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.840
Uh, one for the money or whatever.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.740
But I also can't remember if we have, we had to throw the ball at each other or if you just had to tag someone like they were.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.320
Well, you can tag, but you also can throw.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:02.700
Okay. Maybe that's what it was.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:03.780
Yeah. We called it hot box.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.540
Got it. Totally makes sense.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:12.100
Totally makes sense. Well, we're not talking about real life video games, um, which is weird to call them video games.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.300
They're not, but we're going to talk about Tecmo baseball, the less spoken of Tecmo game.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:22.020
I think we already mentioned this, but I don't think a lot of us were anticipating anything other than Tecmo bowl.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:27.100
Were you guys anticipating like Tecmo to make other sports games on top of the football games?

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:28.400
I was not.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:30.620
I had only heard of a Tecmo bowl.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:37.100
Same. And so the funny thing here is I, I'd give an overview, but we always just say it's baseball.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:38.480
Like, you know what you're getting.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:45.860
However, this time they chose, like Sean mentioned, you know, there, there are choices that you make on where you're going to place the camera and how you're going to play it out.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:58.540
It's kind of a mixture of a lot of the different games that we've seen in the sense that I feel like it kind of plays like RBI baseball in certain respects, but it feels like NES baseball in a lot of other respects.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:01.560
And the camera controls are a lot like bases loaded.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:10.260
So if it's possible to have seen those games get made, learn from them and then make a hybrid of those three games.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.080
I think that's ultimately what Tecmo baseball comes out with.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.560
However, I'm just not sure that the development timeline allows for that.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:26.320
So it's just really bizarre set of coincidences that they managed to copy three different NES baseball games formulas and make a somewhat unique baseball game here.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:37.480
Yeah. I mean, I think part of that is just that there are only so many ways to logically create the sport in a video game sense at this technology level.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:48.020
So, I mean, it wouldn't really make sense to, I don't know, put the camera out to the side so that like the, the pitcher and the batter are like sort of facing each other in profile.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.240
Like that wouldn't make any sense.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.180
So you can really only think like, I'm going to put it in front of the batter.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.840
I'm going to put it in front of the pitcher or something along those lines.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:58.200
Or just like directly overhead.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.400
Or yeah, I guess that is a possibility too.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:09.700
But what's sort of unique about this one is that it just stays behind the pitcher for the most part.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:11.860
Actually entirely.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.280
Regardless of who's batting.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:18.660
Yeah. It takes a broadcast style there.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:26.280
And I think we've talked about that for Bases Loaded, really bringing the presentation part of a baseball game to the NES.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:38.900
Joe, what are your thoughts on when you have that pitcher overhead camera view as opposed to being, you know, the camera kind of behind the catcher where it seems like most baseball games even today have wound up.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:41.120
What's your, what's your take on that camera view?

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.640
You know, it's fine. I didn't really think about it too much.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:47.400
It didn't bother me just because I was just playing this kind of in a vacuum.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:59.020
I, I, I do think that it makes sense that it would be behind the pitcher, whether you're batting or you're pitching, just because if you're playing two player, you would want it to be consistent.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:04.060
And then I just assume they wouldn't want to like go through the trouble of making it different in single player than it would be in two player.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:06.680
But yeah, actually, now that you mentioned it, you're right.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:13.500
That, that does feel weird based on like modern, like MLB games and stuff where it's always behind the catcher.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.600
Right. I, I just hadn't even really thought about it as I was playing, but it's been a while since I've played a baseball game that's not on the NES.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.860
It's almost like the, the, your perspective is the catcher.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.700
Right. Not so much behind, but yeah.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.520
Right, right. That's right. There's also no MLB affiliation in this one.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:34.880
The teams are, you know, loosely mirrored to baseball teams.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:38.240
They just don't use the players' names and they don't quite say New York Mets.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:46.800
I think they say New York NM for national, whatever, not national league, but whatever NM would stand for in this particular league.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.800
New York, New Mexico. There you go.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:54.040
Exactly. It's just a hybrid team between the spring training and the actual place that they play.

00:07:54.040 --> 00:08:01.200
Absolutely. I wanted to ask Sam though, does it matter if we get baseball, real baseball players in these NES games?

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.360
Like they don't really have a chance to get their likeliness in there.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:14.640
So do you really care if you're playing as, uh, you know, the actual stars of 1989 or would you just be fine playing as Derek somebody?

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:23.860
I think that if, especially, I think the only, it doesn't matter except when the pitcher stats come up.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:33.400
I think that might've been cool to see like recognizable pitcher names next to the, like the ones who have good, um, good stats for each game.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.360
And it's funny cause it's there, right?

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:44.240
Like there it's in a way it's the pitcher stats from those teams just not being able to actually use their names.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:56.920
Yeah. I think that if they had, um, I don't know if like between each, uh, each batter, they had like a little portrait that came up to show who was next.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:00.360
Like then it would kind of matter at this point.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:10.420
And this game has, uh, um, it's the, the graphics aren't really all that good in comparison to something like RBI.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:13.220
So that like likeness wouldn't matter anyway.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:24.120
Um, but I think that if, if the game looked a little better and maybe if they had like those portraits that popped up, then, then that would be an interesting thing to do.

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:39.080
Yeah. It also feels like, um, maybe, maybe no one was really thinking of that as much because we're, you mentioned at the top, Mike, that we're like not really in the era yet of, of sequels to baseball games or of like, of like the same company making another baseball game.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:44.000
So it might just feel like if you were to do that, it would, it would be outdated pretty quickly and then be over.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.840
So it might just feel like, uh, kind of pointless to do that.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:53.400
I mean, granted you might still sell, sell in your initial year, but then after a while when, when things are changing, people are moving to different teams or whatever.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.120
Um, totally.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:56.600
It's not like you're coming out with a new one every year.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:10:07.660
Yeah. There's a reason why they do this like yearly release schedule now every year because nobody wants to play with, uh, the New York Mets team that isn't representative of the current team.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:13.500
You know, even though you could technically do those trades yourself, they'd rather just buy the next year's version.

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:20.220
And, uh, we haven't seen that yet on the NES of like a Tecmo baseball, 89 Tecmo baseball, 90, you know?

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:27.520
Right. So when you start up the game, uh, you, you know, it's a confusing set of messages where you have to select twice.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:30.940
They want you to be certain that you're, that you're picking your things.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:32.180
And I'm not going to get into that again.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:39.320
I know how you love that. There's also a confusion for me here with the DH rule of use and not use.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:42.880
Do we think that's just like a, a weird translation thing there?

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:45.680
Like that there could have just been a better way to get that over?

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:49.120
I don't know. The words use and not use just feel strange to me.

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:58.160
I mean, I guess it, I mean, it's not the most, uh, it's not the most elegant way to say it, but it's clear enough to me.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:10:59.680
I would have appreciated a yes.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:00.680
No, that's all I'm saying.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:07.940
Okay. Or maybe just stay true to the fact that in the national league, there is no DH rule.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:12.460
So you don't even need to ask that question if you're playing in a home team's national league ballpark.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:18.040
True. Does it even like, I mean, I guess there's no, there's no differences in the ballpark.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:28.780
So, um, I, well, whoever's the home team, I guess, you know, if the national league team is the home is, is in the bottom of the innings, then you would know like, okay, cool.

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:30.200
No DH rule. I don't know.

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:32.420
It's very specific, but it's a thought I have.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:41.720
I do have bigger thoughts about the game that we can just get into right now.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:46.020
Cause we might as well start talking gameplay, the meat and potatoes of a baseball game.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:49.660
It's all about batting, fielding, base running and pitching.

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:53.900
Those are, those are the meat and potatoes to give a little bit of criticism about the game.

00:11:53.900 --> 00:12:02.400
I think Tecmo baseball finally answered for me why Nintendo didn't trust you to control fielding in NES baseball.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:04.780
Does it have to do with the visibility?

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:07.420
It's a visibility issue.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:14.740
It's also a, just a fielding in general issue is like, I was never able when the opposing team hit the ball.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:24.460
I was never able to accurately predict where that ball was going to land, which one of my fielders was going to be the one, because you just happened to control all of them at the same time.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:30.220
So if you currently see your second baseman and the ball is like a pop-up, you might think like, okay, cool.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:33.960
My second baseman can go like into the shallow outfield and catch that.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:37.080
But then the ball continues to just keep rolling back.

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:40.580
And then all of a sudden you realize that it's more of a catch for your center fielder.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:49.600
However, he's now all the way at the fence because you've been driving your second baseman, left fielder, right fielder, and center fielder all towards the same direction.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:51.940
So now he's got no chance of catching the ball.

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:53.500
The ball just kind of lands there.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:13:02.460
And then it's like an ant, like slowly marching towards the ball, because they are that small, to pick up the ball and then throw it somewhere.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:12.480
So I think maybe that was Nintendo's logic way back when in 1985 to be like, you know, I just don't think we have the technology to let people control the fielders themselves.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:14.340
That's why they made the game haunted.

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:21.620
That's sort of how I started off playing was like that was a very annoying and frustrating feature.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:26.920
And that's sort of how it was, I think, in one of the other baseball games that finally let you control the outfield.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:37.140
But over time, like because the information is there like that, like it still uses that like high pitched whistle sound to tell you when it's still going up.

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:40.360
And it kind of comes back down when the ball starts coming down.

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:50.160
So over the course of the couple of games that I played, I was a bit more accurate with like who I thought the ball was going to.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:58.500
I was never perfect, but I think that like if you played this game over and over, you would sort of get the hang of that.

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:02.360
Yeah, I was I was OK at getting under it.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:13.920
But my problem was that was the visibility thing where like if something if a ball is hit and it's going to left field and it's something that normally I would think the left fielder should get.

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:16.140
But but I can't see the left fielder.

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:21.720
So my gut, my instinct right there is just to have like the shortstop or whoever I can see go towards it.

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:22.640
And then he can't reach it.

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:26.280
And then by the time it by the time it lands, I realize, oh, the left fielder would have been able to get it.

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:30.760
But I would have had no way of knowing it because I don't know where I don't know which I don't know exactly where he is.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:34.260
So it's really hard to control him by guessing where he is off camera.

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:45.640
Yeah, I think that kind of like Sean said, the more that you play this game, the more you get used to you get a feel for that.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:49.460
How? Yeah, because I think for me, the audio cue is the biggest help.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:54.440
Like the you can hear where the peak of it is.

00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:06.760
And so while I also have the same problem with the outfield, I was fine in the infield mostly because of that audio cue.

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:24.180
But I think if I played the game enough to know like, all right, if this is a hit like a fly to left field, I'm not going to send out my shortstop if the pitch is still going up when it is like leaving the infield or something.

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:26.060
You know, yeah, something like that.

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:27.720
But it's not super intuitive.

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:33.340
But then it's like you you you you logically are like, well, I want to control the, you know, the left fielder.

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:37.700
But yeah, of course, you want to bring him in if you if you think you know where it's going to land.

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:40.960
But you still don't know where he is, like left or right of the ball.

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:42.800
Right. That's what was giving me a problem.

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:43.780
Then I'd like get on screen.

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:45.000
I'm like, oh, I'm nowhere near the ball.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:50.300
I think that might come down to just knowing the game and knowing kind of how fast people move.

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:52.720
But still not intuitive at all.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:54.780
Yeah, I sort of agree with that.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:16:00.760
That statement like it's it's not something that you're going to figure out on the first game or two.

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:11.140
But since I'm sure that each setup is exactly the same every time it cuts to that fielding sequence, you will know where your fielders are.

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:16.580
A lot of this game, even, you know, fielding aside, batting, pitching, you name it.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:29.820
A lot of it feels like if I was actually playing in a major league baseball game, the way I was playing this video game was going for me as well as I would expect if I had to take an at bat in the major leagues.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:33.480
You know, the ball, the ball is very fast.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:38.980
And I looked back at some of the other baseball games just to see, like, was it was all the pitching always like this?

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:44.940
Did you always have to be ahead of the of the pitch and really, like, just make your decision on a gut reaction?

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:51.940
And I think RBI baseball comes the closest, although RBI baseball has a little more of an arcadey style to the whole thing.

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:56.900
So I think it works because the whole thing is fast and fun and your bat is huge.

00:16:56.900 --> 00:17:00.400
So a lot of times you don't have to worry about making contact in this game.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:09.480
For some reason, I was just like, I was just really awful at at being able to tell what what the what the pitcher was going to do.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:14.780
And he would he would pretty much be able to paint the strike zone around me and there's no difficulty settings or anything.

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:20.320
I'm just kind of wondering if you guys felt the same way, you know, about batting and stuff like that.

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:24.860
If you felt powerless in there, I'd say, first of all, the umpire is a liar.

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:30.600
And the there's definitely that strike zone is like up to your nose.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:40.780
Um, so there's a lot of because it will telegraph to you where the ball is going to go with the with where the catcher's mitt goes.

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:50.480
And from there, it's just can you get your batter into position and time the and time your swing right?

00:17:50.480 --> 00:18:04.360
Um, but I'd say that the ball moves so fast when it's when they're doing fastballs that like, yeah, this is the hardest I've had to bat in an NES baseball game so far.

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:06.140
It's just very difficult hitting.

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:16.780
I also think that for me, the angle that it like you you hit from the angle that's behind the pitcher.

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:23.420
I understand that it's like for that TV look of getting both in the same shot.

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:31.500
But from a playing experience, I think it's a really frustrating angle to choose because it's like on one.

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:35.960
It's on the Z axis that we don't really know that doesn't really exist.

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:41.900
Um, and two, it's like going away from the camera, if that makes sense, because you're behind the pitcher.

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:45.940
So logically, like the ball should be getting smaller as it approaches the batter.

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:56.580
Whereas in some in like other baseball games where the ball is coming toward you, it's like a little bit easier to time because of that.

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:58.700
But in this one, it's like getting further away.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:00.020
That makes sense.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:01.980
And it's also really fast.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:04.320
Yeah, I get that.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:07.260
That is a kind of unintuitive way to do it.

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:11.780
I didn't have any, um, like save states or anything like that.

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:12.840
So I couldn't confirm this.

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:17.460
But it did feel like certain pitches, you're just not allowed to hit.

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:23.460
There's just something about them where it's like your bat can't line up with where that pitch is going.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:26.540
So if you swing, it's just going to count it as a miss.

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:29.340
It doesn't matter how early, how late you are.

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:31.240
You're not going to get a piece of that.

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:32.720
And obviously, I can't confirm that.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:43.880
I'm not going to try and confirm that, but I will say that I did do some testing because I did do a couple games just playing the game as the game is played.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:48.920
But there were a couple times that I was like, I need to see if I can hit this at all.

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:59.860
And I think I took like 50 chops at this one particular pitch, just rewinding and playing and rewinding and playing while it's in midair.

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:02.520
And I couldn't land on it at all.

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:05.100
So I think that there are some that are just impossible.

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:11.680
Not that anyone that is playing this game on an actual console, like they'd ever know that.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:13.060
Like they would just blame it on themselves.

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:17.280
But I think that there are a couple impossible pitches in this.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:19.020
That's pretty sneaky.

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:23.240
Yeah, because like people, yeah, like you said, people in 89 would never know that.

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:26.400
They probably just thought like, oh, I got to get better at this game.

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:34.320
Well, I'll admit something when it comes to the batting that after a while, because I was having a lot of trouble with it too.

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:43.620
And after a while, I noticed that, I just noticed that for some reason, there were a lot of like, apparently just at the top of the strike zone pitches.

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:47.020
Like, I don't know, it seemed like there were more than any other pitches.

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:51.420
So I started just before the pitch was even thrown, just deciding I'm going to swing high.

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:59.800
And I had better results just by guessing high every time than I did when I was actually trying to like, no, like trying to hit the pitches.

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:01.640
So I don't know what that tells you about my skill.

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:05.640
Hey, I mean, you found a pattern and you exploited it.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:14.760
Do you think that, you know, given that the game seems to be kind of hard to a novice, right?

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:21.340
Do you think that with time, I don't know how much time it would take, but just pretend that you have time.

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:31.560
Do you think that this game could replicate the more fun baseball experiences that we're used to having in some of our other NES games?

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:39.000
Or is this one, is this one just being a little too difficult to continue on with?

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:48.080
I think notwithstanding the handful of impossible pitches, I think that this does come pretty close.

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:55.720
Because in previous games, I could not for the life of me pull off any kind of play in terms of base running.

00:21:56.020 --> 00:22:14.460
But with the base running system in this game, like I was able to, you know, send my first base runner to second and then swing away and actually sort of get myself out of what would have been a double play.

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:25.960
And then likewise, on the other hand, like in terms of fielding, I was able to actually pick up some double plays that in previous games just felt impossible to me.

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:33.160
Because I feel like in this game, the fielders have a little bit more leeway with timing.

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:43.020
So I think that like the way that they tweak the knobs in this one for balance, there are some like fun, there's some fun stuff that you can do with it.

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:54.180
I actually did notice that you mentioned too, I noticed a couple times where a fielder, an AI fielder would just hold the ball for like two seconds when they clearly could have gotten me out.

00:22:54.460 --> 00:23:00.180
Yeah. I don't know if that was just like a little bit of a, like a mercy thing that they put into the game to make it out too hard.

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:02.420
But I noticed that a couple times.

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:09.920
There is something though about pace of play in a baseball game, in a baseball video game too, where there, you know,

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:21.220
and this game had a weird balance of it too, because you have the pitcher throws the ball really fast, which I think, you know, hey, that's probably how fast it, or if not faster, it feels in the real major leagues, right?

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:23.920
Those guys are throwing 96, 98 miles an hour.

00:23:24.220 --> 00:23:26.800
You know, who knows what it's like to face that kind of at bat?

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:28.380
Only the other major leaguers do.

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:31.720
So it has, it has like moments like that.

00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:37.720
But the fielding thing is a little weird, because Joe, as you mentioned, they'll hold on to the ball, which I think is, you know, forgivable, right?

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:39.940
It's like, great, I'd love to be safe sometimes.

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:44.200
But also like to, for the fielding of your own part, right?

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:47.440
Forget the argument about, you know, sound cues and stuff like that.

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:51.220
Fielders in general just move pretty slow in this game.

00:23:51.220 --> 00:24:02.620
And if the ball gets hit in certain areas, it is a crawl to get to the ball and then throw it to likely third base, because you know that the guy hit a triple at this point.

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:16.340
There's something about like how, in this style of a video game, there needs to be a decision about like, how fast an inning should move, and how long a game of baseball should take to play.

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:28.820
I think Bases Loaded really got that right, in the sense that it was going full-on simulation and was trying to experience like a very long baseball game that was also, you know, had its downsides, right?

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:33.420
It wasn't entirely fun, and we ultimately didn't put it on the essential games list, but it stuck to that.

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:39.880
And then you have RBI Baseball, which was very arcade-y, and I think a lot of us enjoyed, but still didn't get on the essential games list.

00:24:39.880 --> 00:24:48.220
This game feels like it's picking and choosing its pace throughout based on whether you're fielding, batting, pitching, or running.

00:24:48.420 --> 00:25:01.500
And I actually do agree with Sean that the running part is probably the most fluid of any of the running systems we've had so far, but also the most enjoyable because you actually feel like you have some control on the base running in the sense of,

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:10.960
okay, well, I can send this guy, but then if it looks like he's going to be out, I can send him back, and then I can put these guys in a squeeze and grab another guy over to third base.

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.860
I like stuff like that, so I appreciate that.

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:17.180
That's the kind of stuff I wanted to see modeled throughout the rest of the game.

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:20.580
So I have a little bit of a devil's advocate on the running, though.

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:36.960
It is just one flaw, but it's a flaw that kind of hurt me quite a bit, and that's that when there's like a pop fly, anyone you have on base will automatically just run to the next base, will just advance.

00:25:39.620 --> 00:25:43.860
And at that point, if the ball is caught, it's just like an easy triple play.

00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:52.320
You know, it's just too easy to get everyone out because they don't, I mean, as far as I know, there's not an option to have them run halfway down the bases and wait.

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:54.340
So they just run or go back.

00:25:54.660 --> 00:25:59.600
There's no in-between like you would do in a real baseball game and just wait to see if the ball is caught.

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:05.700
And a couple times I just didn't even notice that was happening because I was paying attention to my runner or whatever, and I didn't notice that was happening.

00:26:06.680 --> 00:26:12.940
That or I didn't want to like give up and go all the way back for all my runners.

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:14.640
No, I totally get that.

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:16.360
Yeah, that's a huge flaw.

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:23.240
Yeah, as soon as the ball is caught, it's just like, well, now I'm screwed because everybody who was on base is out because there's no getting back.

00:26:23.740 --> 00:26:25.500
I had a problem with it at first.

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:34.180
A joke is I noticed that actually happened to me with like I had a guy on third and it was only one out and it was a clear pop up and I was like caught by surprise with it.

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:42.180
But then I kind of thought like, well, you know, the alternative is no, you know, in this baseball game, obviously later baseball games can do whatever they want.

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:46.740
But in this one, the alternative is they just never run and you have to send them all the time.

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:57.720
And I think it's better to be in control of because once you've hit the ball, your only responsibility now as the offensive team is to just be in charge of base running.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:10.660
It should be your call to send people back rather than say, OK, I'm going to send everyone forward because then you're losing momentum on a potential like extra base hit when they're not running.

00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:13.320
And you have to send them like you'd have to make that call then.

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:23.080
So I did appreciate that they decided to make everybody run rather than not run, because I think you would be caught in more jams if the bases were loaded, if it was the opposite way.

00:27:23.080 --> 00:27:33.480
I don't think it's binary, though, because I think that there there is the like they could have put some game logic into this that recognizes that that hit is a pop up.

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:40.920
And in that regard, base running will behave differently like it does in modern games.

00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:46.300
It doesn't have to just always be they stay until told to run or they run.

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:49.580
Oh, absolutely. That would have that would have been the best solution.

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:55.560
Hands down. I just think like if the only option is on or off, I'm saying leave that let them run.

00:27:55.660 --> 00:27:56.720
Yeah, I agree with that.

00:27:57.140 --> 00:28:04.560
One thing that it made me think of is my favorite baseball games were always the backyard baseball games.

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:12.460
And the way that those guys do it is that if there's a pop fly, all of the base runners will still run, but they'll run really slowly.

00:28:14.080 --> 00:28:18.260
And then after it's caught, they'll automatically do what they're supposed to do.

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:26.320
But you can also tell them individually to do like to advance if you want to risk it or you can go back to a base.

00:28:26.320 --> 00:28:32.380
So I think that that is like a pretty simple, maybe way.

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:37.820
I don't know how to code it, but that seems like it's a good halfway or a good solution.

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:43.460
In between innings, you're greeted on the scoreboard with just the words fight.

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:45.900
Not all the time.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:47.460
Not all the time.

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:52.620
Sure, sure. But like that one in specific, I guess I see what message they're going for.

00:28:52.620 --> 00:28:58.180
But if we're going for like baseball fanfare, shouldn't it be like charge instead of fight?

00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:02.520
I mean, I think I saw fight a couple times, but most of the time it was just move on.

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:07.080
And it would move on to the next inning.

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:13.480
And I think sometimes when maybe there was a home run in the previous inning, it would say like big hit.

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:14.900
Yeah, I got that one too.

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:18.380
Otherwise, they're kind of like non sequitur, and I kind of love it for that.

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:21.100
They also have Tecmo is number one.

00:29:21.100 --> 00:29:23.360
Yeah, it's usually the seventh inning, I think.

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:34.200
I also brought up fight just because the first time that it happened, I was a little startled and thought that there was going to be like a benches clearing drawl that was happening between innings.

00:29:34.340 --> 00:29:35.500
I would have loved that too.

00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:42.720
But that's why I just thought like, you know, fight is something that you would expect to see definitely in a hockey game, in a football game, sure.

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:46.480
But in a baseball game, like fights don't happen that often.

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:49.740
So it just seemed really strange when it popped up on the scoreboard.

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:58.340
Now that you say it like that, I want to see like, I want to see the NFL referees like allowing two football players to fight just on the field.

00:29:58.520 --> 00:29:59.480
That would be something else.

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:04.280
It really does show you how weird it is that it's kind of allowed in hockey.

00:30:04.420 --> 00:30:05.840
But that's beside the point.

00:30:11.580 --> 00:30:13.520
And so let's move on to pitching.

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:16.080
Probably the only thing we haven't really talked in depth about yet.

00:30:16.540 --> 00:30:26.140
This one lets you choose your style of pitch, but then also control that pitch with your cursor as the pitch moves, you know, into the strike zone.

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:27.380
Did we like that?

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:28.840
I did like that.

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:34.420
So it sort of works like there's like three phases to each pitch.

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:44.000
When you initiate the pitch, whether you're holding up, down or nothing on the D-pad, that sort of controls the speed.

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:48.020
So it could be like a fastball or like a slower pitch.

00:30:49.280 --> 00:31:02.200
And then while the animation of the pitcher pitching is going on, you can select, I think, like eight different directions of like what corner you want to hit.

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:10.640
And then while the ball is in the air, you could do like that aftertouch to make a curve in one way or another.

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:26.020
So I think that like pitching in this game, at least in terms of how much control you have over each pitch, is probably the best executed, at least in my memory, of the baseball games we've played so far.

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:28.140
Yeah, I kind of agree with that, too.

00:31:28.140 --> 00:31:34.160
I, when I, my first thought was it was weird that you're controlling the ball while it's in the air.

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:35.820
I don't remember if any other games did that.

00:31:36.500 --> 00:31:46.480
And while obviously that's, that like kind of requires a set of like a skill or a type of thinking that isn't how you would think while you're pitching.

00:31:46.600 --> 00:31:49.180
Like once the ball's in the air, you don't have control over it anymore.

00:31:49.340 --> 00:31:56.600
But it is, when I thought about it more, it is a nice way to kind of replace the skill it would take to actually throw a curveball or actually, you know,

00:31:56.600 --> 00:32:00.780
it's not just like select curveball and your pitcher will automatically throw a curveball.

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:02.240
Like you have to be doing something.

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:08.560
And the best way to translate that is by, that I can think of is by allowing you to control the ball in the air, if that makes any sense.

00:32:08.780 --> 00:32:17.840
Yeah, I think there was one, one, I don't remember which, but one of the more basic looking baseball games we played.

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:23.160
I think all of the control was sort of after, after the pitch, but.

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:29.080
Exactly. I, I think that there are others that didn't allow you to do that at all.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.000
Yeah. And it is an interesting decision to have like, first you choose speed, right?

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:53.460
Then only afterwards you're choosing, like Sean mentioned, the eight different directions, but also like the, the curve of the ball, like whether it will curve right or left or even forking the ball so that it has this sort of downwards curve, which I know fooled me every time the actual computer AI pulled it off.

00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:58.040
Yeah. That's really all you can say about pitching, right?

00:32:58.200 --> 00:33:01.100
I'm not sure there, there's a lot else there.

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:13.200
Otherwise, I definitely thought this was the more fun part of the game as opposed to batting because in batting, it was just so stressful to try and hit that ball.

00:33:13.340 --> 00:33:22.360
And honestly, I was, even if, even if they were, forget about if the ump is rigged or not and their strikes or balls more than likely, I was just swinging to hope to make some kind of contact.

00:33:22.360 --> 00:33:24.840
And then let's just see what happens when the ball is in the field.

00:33:25.020 --> 00:33:28.780
Yeah. You know, like let's just see what happens, but it wasn't good if I chose not to swing.

00:33:28.940 --> 00:33:35.040
Although I do wonder, do you think like, do you think a walk is possible with an umpire that rigged?

00:33:35.120 --> 00:33:37.380
I was walked a couple of times in my play.

00:33:37.380 --> 00:33:38.500
Okay. That's good to know. Yeah.

00:33:38.840 --> 00:33:40.040
I just swang all the time.

00:33:40.200 --> 00:33:44.040
I have to swing when I see the ball come.

00:33:44.160 --> 00:33:45.260
There's just something about it.

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:50.320
I don't know if it's that view, like that it would be impossible for me to know if it's a ball or not.

00:33:50.320 --> 00:33:51.960
I thought it was a personality trait of yours.

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:54.480
Yeah. Sean, I think it's secretly a personality trait.

00:33:54.560 --> 00:33:57.420
Sean, how did you know that they were going to be balls?

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:07.880
I just, I mean like, throughout the game, I could have sworn that the same upstairs pitch was called a strike and a ball at random.

00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:14.880
So I just sort of hoped for the best and, you know, was swinging at fouls that were obvious in the zone.

00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:20.640
And I was able to drag like these plate appearances out until I somehow got walked.

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:25.140
Well, you know, random calls in the same pitch position.

00:34:25.140 --> 00:34:26.900
That sounds like a simulation game now.

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:29.720
That's very real to what happens in a regular baseball game.

00:34:29.720 --> 00:34:32.060
So, you know, no complaints there.

00:34:32.240 --> 00:34:36.700
We also have a watch mode, just a mode where you could just watch Tecmo baseball.

00:34:37.180 --> 00:34:42.140
Do you think that this is something you would just like, you know, oh, hey, let me plug in the cartridge real fast.

00:34:42.140 --> 00:34:43.940
We'll just put on the watch mode and have a party.

00:34:44.380 --> 00:34:46.660
Yeah. A certain kind of person that does that.

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:48.200
I don't like that person.

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:51.400
Like in the off season, you want to watch some baseball.

00:34:52.940 --> 00:34:56.400
If you want to like play some bets, I guess, with your friends.

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:03.440
I remember back in the day, it was probably like the Xbox era Madden games.

00:35:03.600 --> 00:35:15.360
Like they did have like a camera angle that was sort of like that somewhat isometric view of the field that sort of mimicked the press box angle.

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:21.000
And I tried to watch a computer controlled Madden game once.

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:23.780
And the first time I did it, I'm like, this isn't working for me.

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:25.560
This looks stupid.

00:35:25.800 --> 00:35:27.520
And I'm never doing this again.

00:35:28.640 --> 00:35:30.020
I've heard. We found out.

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:36.400
We found out that a in Mario Party, if the computers are all like turned on to play.

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:40.880
Right. So you can have four computers just watch them play like a 50 turn Mario Party.

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:43.320
Right. You could just go do whatever else you want.

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:52.140
But if you on the last turn change them to be human controllers, then you get all the stars that they got during those 50 turns.

00:35:52.220 --> 00:35:58.380
So this way you could cash those in to progress along with the rest of the game for the hundred stars required to get to the ending.

00:35:59.320 --> 00:36:00.720
And so we did that.

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:03.800
We would just like have them run and the computers would do that.

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:05.400
And we weren't actually going to watch it.

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:12.660
I think it was bizarre that Nintendo included a feature that would just let you watch four computers play Mario Party for 50 turns.

00:36:13.440 --> 00:36:20.240
But then there's also the risk of like, you know, you go outside, you do some stuff, you come back and they're actually like done, you know, and you missed it.

00:36:20.380 --> 00:36:21.700
And now you have to run it again.

00:36:22.460 --> 00:36:24.560
Incredible. I cannot believe you did that.

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:35.220
I know that there's like charity melee tournaments also where everyone like pays money to be a computer player and then all the computers just fight each other.

00:36:35.440 --> 00:36:39.040
So no one's actually fighting, but they still do it tournament style.

00:36:39.300 --> 00:36:44.020
And certain characters cost more money because they have better odds than other characters do.

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:49.420
So I think that sometimes it could be maybe interesting.

00:36:50.420 --> 00:37:03.840
Yeah, that sounds fun. The Smash Brothers thing just reminded me of when the Amiibos came out and people were setting up on Twitch like eight player Amiibo fights because the Amiibos allegedly could learn from each other and from playing with you.

00:37:03.840 --> 00:37:05.920
So that's how they'd level up and they'd learn what to do.

00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:10.380
They would host these on Twitch and then you could bet on who you were thinking was going to win.

00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:15.500
And the better the win to loss ratio of the Amiibo was, the worse the payout was.

00:37:15.500 --> 00:37:20.920
So that was like actually very interesting and a good version of a watch mode.

00:37:21.180 --> 00:37:29.640
But, you know, I just think in general, like watch modes are just something that's so bizarre to even just see on these NES games.

00:37:29.740 --> 00:37:41.180
Like the idea of a child in this time wanting to watch something happen rather than just play it themselves feels weird to include.

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:49.480
It's funny, though, because now, I mean, obviously there's personalities attached, but now that's what everyone always does is watch other people play video games on YouTube.

00:37:49.700 --> 00:37:50.780
So they were ahead of the time.

00:37:51.020 --> 00:37:52.260
At least they're people, you know? Right, yeah.

00:37:52.300 --> 00:37:53.620
At least there's some personality there.

00:37:53.840 --> 00:38:01.060
Yeah, maybe we should do a Twitch stream with the four of us doing audio commentary of a watch mode Tecmo baseball game.

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:02.940
That would be great. I'm not against this, yeah.

00:38:03.140 --> 00:38:06.860
One of us is just going to have to be like the injury report person, though.

00:38:08.500 --> 00:38:13.960
That's great. So we're kind of winding down here.

00:38:14.060 --> 00:38:15.920
Let me take you through sequels and spinoffs.

00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:20.940
We're going to get a bunch of other Tecmo games on the NES, including Tecmo Bowl.

00:38:21.140 --> 00:38:22.380
I think that's pretty famous.

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:23.840
I think everybody expected that one.

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:25.600
Then Tecmo Super Bowl.

00:38:25.840 --> 00:38:30.240
I don't know if that was like a surprise, but seems like the next natural step to go.

00:38:30.560 --> 00:38:35.900
Seems like it would take a lot of money, though, to get the word Super Bowl included in there.

00:38:35.980 --> 00:38:37.700
I know you're not really allowed to say that anyone.

00:38:37.700 --> 00:38:41.640
Like the NFL has the rights to the words Super Bowl in that order.

00:38:41.980 --> 00:38:43.720
Yeah, you shouldn't even be saying it right now.

00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:44.860
It's weird, though, right?

00:38:44.920 --> 00:38:46.160
Because technically I'm like promoting it.

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:47.860
What do they have against using it?

00:38:48.340 --> 00:38:52.220
Like technically me saying the Super Bowl right now, everybody's like, oh, yeah, that exists and I should watch it in February.

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:53.900
Now we're going to get it.

00:38:54.140 --> 00:38:55.400
I forgot it existed, Mike.

00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:58.520
Anyway, there's also Tecmo NBA basketball.

00:38:58.520 --> 00:39:05.660
Now that seems like they're going to have players from the NBA or at least teams because they're using the NBA thing.

00:39:06.620 --> 00:39:08.960
And Tecmo Cup soccer game.

00:39:09.160 --> 00:39:13.320
Do you think they needed to include the subtitle soccer game?

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:16.640
Does that feel weird? I thought you just included that just now.

00:39:17.120 --> 00:39:18.760
Right? Yeah. No, that's it.

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:19.900
That is really there.

00:39:20.080 --> 00:39:22.640
It could be a racing game, right?

00:39:23.080 --> 00:39:26.260
That's true. Or even a tennis game, I suppose.

00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:29.900
It could be like, what's that pouring beer game?

00:39:30.040 --> 00:39:31.360
It could be like pouring things into cups.

00:39:31.520 --> 00:39:33.020
What the heck is that game? Arcade game.

00:39:34.920 --> 00:39:36.700
Tapper? Yes.

00:39:36.700 --> 00:39:38.720
Or not Tapper. Something like that.

00:39:39.020 --> 00:39:40.280
Wow. It could be that.

00:39:40.400 --> 00:39:41.580
It's not. Okay.

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:42.880
What is it?

00:39:43.120 --> 00:39:45.520
Soccer game. Yeah, it's a soccer game.

00:39:46.260 --> 00:39:49.500
Oh, I thought we were talking about the beer game again.

00:39:49.960 --> 00:39:51.280
No, we're saying Tapper.

00:39:51.620 --> 00:39:52.440
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:39:53.160 --> 00:39:54.480
It's okay. You said it.

00:39:55.280 --> 00:39:56.440
All right.

00:39:56.780 --> 00:39:58.460
Speaking of you said it.

00:39:58.640 --> 00:40:04.420
There's also a Tecmo baseball game on the Super Nintendo and the Sega Genesis.

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:07.660
Can we guess what they might have called that game?

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:09.700
Tecmo Super Baseball?

00:40:09.700 --> 00:40:10.580
Super Tecmo.

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:11.980
You are absolutely right.

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:14.700
Oh, Sam, you're so close, though.

00:40:14.820 --> 00:40:18.380
It might make more sense to be Super Tecmo Baseball.

00:40:18.380 --> 00:40:23.940
Because contrary to popular belief, there is no Super Baseball championship at the end.

00:40:24.040 --> 00:40:25.240
Like, there is a Super Bowl.

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:27.440
There's the World Series instead.

00:40:27.740 --> 00:40:30.040
So, Tecmo Super Baseball is what they went with.

00:40:30.120 --> 00:40:32.320
But maybe they should have went with Super Tecmo Baseball.

00:40:32.420 --> 00:40:35.000
Feels like the natural sequel to Tecmo Baseball.

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:45.040
I took a chance here and said, well, if I'm going to continue the streak of playing all these sequels and spinoff games for the 1989 games, I might as well play Tecmo Super Baseball.

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:48.740
This is a game that is very advanced for its time.

00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:52.900
I was blown away by some of the features that they included in this game.

00:40:53.320 --> 00:41:01.400
And then I was, like, simultaneously blown away by how many obtuse decisions are included in this game.

00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:05.900
It's almost like they knew that Tecmo Baseball was sort of a tough game.

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:08.600
Where, like, yeah, batting's not that much fun, but pitching's okay.

00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:11.600
Fielding is slow, but base running is fast.

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:16.260
It feels like they knew that that happened, and then they just doubled down on it on the Super Nintendo.

00:41:17.540 --> 00:41:22.480
And honestly, like, I consider Tecmo Super Baseball unplayable by my standards.

00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:30.020
I don't think that that's actually, like, that's like baseball if you're dreaming, you know, and you're just, like, running in slow motion.

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:34.980
This is, like, everything that you try to do in that game, it's just a game where you can't win.

00:41:35.080 --> 00:41:41.180
Any time that Mike refers to his dreams, I feel bad for him because they sound so nightmarish.

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:42.880
All the dreams are nightmarish.

00:41:42.880 --> 00:41:52.240
It really is. But I would argue that dreaming you're playing in the major leagues and playing in the actual major leagues would be very similar experiences.

00:41:52.820 --> 00:41:55.620
Okay. You know, like, you're going to be bad.

00:41:56.180 --> 00:41:59.160
Is that how it works in professional baseball?

00:41:59.460 --> 00:42:00.580
Or professional dreams?

00:42:01.820 --> 00:42:03.500
Professional dreams and professional baseball.

00:42:03.720 --> 00:42:06.800
Think about it, Sean. You step into the major leagues by accident, right?

00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:11.980
You're the rookie of the year, that movie where the Chicago Cubs kid has broken his arm or whatever.

00:42:12.080 --> 00:42:13.060
You're that kid. Okay.

00:42:13.100 --> 00:42:15.400
But you also don't have that superpower.

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:17.380
That happens in the movie. Instead, you're just playing.

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:19.360
No, I know, I know. Yeah, exactly.

00:42:19.520 --> 00:42:20.920
It's true. It did happen in the movie.

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:26.200
But just pretend for a minute, Sean, that it's you at your current age with all your current abilities.

00:42:26.400 --> 00:42:28.600
And they're telling you, you just have to pinch it.

00:42:28.760 --> 00:42:30.720
Okay? Oh, God. It's no big deal.

00:42:31.280 --> 00:42:32.600
Middle of the, you know, like.

00:42:33.400 --> 00:42:35.680
Are you insulting me right now, Mike?

00:42:35.800 --> 00:42:37.140
I feel it. No, it's the middle of the season.

00:42:37.400 --> 00:42:39.060
Fourth inning of a know-nothing game.

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:40.080
Like, it doesn't matter at all.

00:42:40.080 --> 00:42:44.960
But now you know that you have to go up there and at least see three pitches.

00:42:45.540 --> 00:42:47.180
You know, like, do you even swing?

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:48.260
Do you even go for it?

00:42:48.300 --> 00:42:49.720
I think I'd fucking go for it.

00:42:49.760 --> 00:42:53.000
You gotta go for it. I know I wouldn't hit, but I think I'd fucking go for it.

00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:53.920
I bet I'd hit it.

00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:59.900
Swing away, Mike. I bet I would swing around in a full circle and then fall into the dirt.

00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:02.320
I bet I'd get thrown out.

00:43:02.320 --> 00:43:05.160
Yeah, I'd swing like a half hour after the ball was in the catcher's mitt.

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:08.540
Anyway, we're not here to talk about how we would perform in the major leagues.

00:43:08.540 --> 00:43:15.260
We're here to talk about how Tecmo baseball would perform in a little something I like to call the Essential Games List.

00:43:17.920 --> 00:43:24.840
So, to be honest, I don't really know where on my baseball games list this falls exactly.

00:43:25.500 --> 00:43:33.520
I feel like they're all getting, I mean, not that they're like, for all emulating the same sport, they have their differences.

00:43:33.520 --> 00:43:36.720
But in my head, they're all bleeding together a little bit.

00:43:36.720 --> 00:43:45.500
But what I can tell you is that this game is definitely not significantly higher than the other ones enough to get it on the Essential Games List.

00:43:45.680 --> 00:43:52.520
So, for all the same reasons I haven't voted any baseball games so far on the Essential Games List, I'm also not voting this under the Essential Games List.

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:58.780
And Sam? This game is not the most fun baseball game that I've played.

00:43:59.020 --> 00:44:01.880
I don't think it's the most fun baseball game I've played on the NES.

00:44:02.320 --> 00:44:05.620
And there's a lot of stuff that's wrong with it.

00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:06.820
There's a lot of frustrating parts.

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:18.420
Personally, I think that for a baseball game on a console like the NES to be successful, it has to be way more arcade-y and less realistic to be fun.

00:44:19.040 --> 00:44:26.140
And so this game, I don't know, it simulates baseball well enough, but it's not on the Essential Games List for me.

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:28.420
Fair enough. Sean?

00:44:29.280 --> 00:44:35.820
I'll agree with Sam in that this isn't even the best baseball game on the system so far.

00:44:37.000 --> 00:44:47.300
I think it's up there, but just by virtue of not being good enough at the only thing it can do, it's not going to be on the Essential Games List.

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:56.320
Yeah, you're kind of doomed if you are in a genre that currently has no games on the Essential Games List, and then you're not the best game in that genre.

00:44:56.520 --> 00:44:59.140
Like, it's going to be really hard to get on the Essential Games List.

00:44:59.220 --> 00:45:00.980
So it's no surprise the way we voted today.

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:17.380
I would say, however, that I could put this above NES Baseball, close to bases loaded, right up there with Major League Baseball, but like RBI Baseball is still far and away the best one on the system.

00:45:17.680 --> 00:45:24.420
You know, if NES Baseball is haunted, I think the Tecmo Baseball has like slight levels of paranormal activity.

00:45:25.600 --> 00:45:30.400
Like, there's moments where this game has a mind of its own.

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:35.220
Certainly the ump does, but I just, I don't want to say it's haunted.

00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:39.120
And I almost, like, when I first started playing this game, it felt like hell.

00:45:39.880 --> 00:45:48.920
But at the end of the day, given its shakeout, I think, like, there's just slight moments of paranormal activity that would set off, you know.

00:45:49.060 --> 00:45:51.920
Like an EVP device or something like that, you know?

00:45:52.140 --> 00:45:53.400
Exactly. Yeah. Exactly.

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:55.900
Nothing to be alarmed about.

00:45:56.200 --> 00:45:57.960
It's fine if you have a copy in your house.

00:45:58.260 --> 00:46:00.340
I wouldn't throw it out just yet.

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:02.960
Just don't put it on top of a Ouija board.

00:46:03.900 --> 00:46:04.780
Yes, exactly.

00:46:05.080 --> 00:46:05.960
And don't speak to it.

00:46:07.320 --> 00:46:09.580
Don't speak to any of your video games, though, honestly.

00:46:10.740 --> 00:46:16.640
Next week, we're going to keep up the sports genre, and we're going to play WWF WrestleMania.

00:46:16.840 --> 00:46:22.400
I can only imagine how many sports fans I upset by calling a WWF game a sports game.

00:46:22.400 --> 00:46:23.560
But I just did.

00:46:23.700 --> 00:46:25.880
And there's nothing you can do about it because it's been recorded.

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:28.260
Sean, is WWF a sport?

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:32.200
Wow. That's a loaded question.

00:46:32.260 --> 00:46:33.440
I don't think so.

00:46:33.720 --> 00:46:35.560
It's entertainment. Sports entertainment.

00:46:36.340 --> 00:46:38.020
I thought it was a federation.

00:46:38.420 --> 00:46:39.620
Well, that's interesting.

00:46:39.840 --> 00:46:48.140
Yeah, that's true. I don't think we say, though, that baseball is a league before we say it's a sport.

00:46:49.220 --> 00:46:50.500
Well, no, no, no.

00:46:50.600 --> 00:46:53.900
You asked me, is WWF a sport?

00:46:54.040 --> 00:46:58.140
That's true. I didn't want to say wrestling, though, because then it's like, well, which kind, right?

00:46:58.220 --> 00:47:00.980
Because there's obviously wrestling that is totally a sport.

00:47:01.220 --> 00:47:02.080
And then there's...

00:47:02.080 --> 00:47:04.600
No, I think even, like, Greco-Roman wrestling is rigged.

00:47:05.560 --> 00:47:08.960
Interesting. Well, I mean, we don't have any way to find out whether or not, right?

00:47:10.760 --> 00:47:14.660
Like, who knows if the Olympics might be scripted, for all we know.

00:47:14.840 --> 00:47:18.400
Did Michael Phelps really win all those medals by that slim of a margin?

00:47:20.060 --> 00:47:22.820
Or did they start turning on the water jets for the other guys?

00:47:22.820 --> 00:47:25.920
I think this is the only worthwhile conspiracy theory.

00:47:26.160 --> 00:47:32.460
Just saying. WWF WrestleMania, it's a big deal because we've been talking about...

00:47:32.460 --> 00:47:33.320
I've been talking about it.

00:47:33.320 --> 00:47:35.680
I don't know if you guys have. I've been talking about WWF for a while.

00:47:35.680 --> 00:47:38.940
And now I get to finally talk about a video game for WWF.

00:47:39.120 --> 00:47:43.740
We've played some other wrestling games before, like Muscle in 1986.

00:47:44.420 --> 00:47:45.580
That was a mess.

00:47:45.980 --> 00:47:53.180
So we're going to see if WWF WrestleMania could either break that streak or take the title.

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:55.360
I really just want to play as Sting.

00:47:55.820 --> 00:47:59.840
Sting is WCW, but I can understand how you would make that confusion.

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:01.200
No, no, no. It's not confusion.

00:48:01.820 --> 00:48:03.640
I just want to play as Sting.

00:48:04.040 --> 00:48:04.960
Oh, I got you.

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:06.200
So this game can't be on...

00:48:06.200 --> 00:48:09.620
You judge your Wrestling Essential Games list by whether Sting is in it or not.

00:48:11.320 --> 00:48:12.400
You got it.

00:48:13.040 --> 00:48:14.400
Yeah, that's true.

00:48:14.740 --> 00:48:16.500
Sting the wrestler or Sting from the place?

00:48:16.500 --> 00:48:17.440
That's what I was going to ask.

00:48:17.560 --> 00:48:18.440
I wasn't sure about that.

00:48:19.700 --> 00:48:21.520
Sting the wrestler.

00:48:23.840 --> 00:48:27.240
He's going to say NWO is going down.

00:48:27.460 --> 00:48:29.460
He should say, like, don't stand so close to me.

00:48:31.620 --> 00:48:32.880
That would have been good, too.

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:38.800
Joe, any final thoughts on wrestling, Tecmo Baseball, the podcast, where to find us?

00:48:38.980 --> 00:48:39.860
Any thoughts about that?

00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:46.460
As far as wrestling, I just hope it's as good as the PlayStation 1 WWF games, which I'm sure

00:48:46.460 --> 00:48:47.320
is exactly the same.

00:48:47.320 --> 00:48:49.460
That's a great call, though.

00:48:49.580 --> 00:48:51.980
I love those PlayStation 1 WWF games.

00:48:52.220 --> 00:48:56.000
Me, too. So I have a high stakes or a high bar set for next week.

00:48:56.180 --> 00:48:59.500
That's true. Anyway, since you couldn't answer it, I will.

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:03.040
You can always find us at NostalgiaCast on Twitter.

00:49:03.260 --> 00:49:05.440
You can talk to me, Michael Esposito.

00:49:05.720 --> 00:49:07.900
I'm at Esposito Film on Twitter.

00:49:07.900 --> 00:49:40.100
And we'll be talking to you next Friday for another great episode of Nostalgia. NostalgiaCast on Twitter.
